First N where a PRODUCT construction strictly beats 3N: 32
Product TIES (cap == 3N) at N = [27, 30]

==============================================================================
THE N=27 = 3^3 TIE, explicitly:
   K3 [] K3 [] K3  (Cartesian CUBE of the triangle)  N=27, edges=81=3*27, avgdeg=6/1
   G9 [] K3        (optimal 9-pt graph x triangle)   N=27, edges=81=3*27, avgdeg=6/1

Both hit avgdeg = 6 EXACTLY (=3+3 or 2+2+2) -> u(27) >= 81 by products,
and NO product can exceed it (additivity caps avgdeg at 2+2+2=6 for 3^3).

==============================================================================
CONSEQUENCE for N* in [25,28] (THM-431):
   N=25: product cap = 70 <= 3N=75   | proven u(N) lower bound (Moser, NON-product) = 75
   N=26: product cap = 73 <= 3N=78   | proven u(N) lower bound (Moser, NON-product) = 78
   N=27: product cap = 81 <= 3N=81   | proven u(N) lower bound (Moser, NON-product) = 81
   N=28: product cap = 83 <= 3N=84   | proven u(N) lower bound (Moser, NON-product) = 85

Since the product cap <= 3N for every N <= 31 (tie at most), ANY graph with
u(N) > 3N at N <= 31 -- in particular at the crossover N* in [25,28] -- is
NECESSARILY NON-PRODUCT (irreducible).  N* is a Moser-lattice phenomenon;
the Erdos product family alone first beats 3N only at N = 32 .

TANGENCY + TAIL:
  P(N)-3N grazes 0 (tangent from below) at N=27,30 (the ties), dips to -1 at
  N=28 between them, then crosses positive at 32 and stays positive:
  every composite N in [32,42] strictly beats 3N: True
  -> the crossover N* in [25,28] sits exactly in the grazing region (tie@27,
     dip@28) where the product family touches but cannot cross 3N.
